resident evil village isn't just your  run-of-the-mill horror game  it's a bit more involved there's hidden  treasure a merchant  hunting inventory management and  exploration  if you're new to the series or even  survival horror in general  then welcome these tips will help you  find your footing  rather than trembling around every  corner in fear in this video i'm going  to break it down into three  sections combat exploration and the duke  where i'll provide tips and tricks in  each category you can also  find stamps below combat tip one  go for the knees i know you're going to  want to be a hot shot with your head  shots but in resident evil  going for the dome isn't always the best  option the game's werewolf-like enemies  lichens are unruly fairly unpredictable  dudes that scour the village  they'll go from gaining real quick on  you to slowly stalking you as they get  closer and closer but don't be  fooled aim for the head and they'll duck  and juke out of the way leading to some  wasted rounds  it's annoying instead a few shots to the  knees will bring them down along for  opportune head shooting  the hitbox on their legs is also much  more forgiving making it easier to aim  down and fire without getting too  precise about it and all the same  principles apply to these  cave dwelling ghoulies sort of dudes  just keep in mind that there's a variety  of enemies in village  some of which are more spongy while  others wear armor so this tip  isn't universal however you'll be  fighting lycans and ghouls the most  so this strategy will be the most  exhausted in your encounters  tip 2 time your counters if you block an  attack  you have a small window of time to press  block again and trigger a counter  that will kick your enemy back which  also disarms them as well as having a  knockback on ones around you  in some cases when you're surrounded and  overwhelmed deliberately taking the hit  blocking it and countering it pays off  in disarming an enemy  and staggering others to go in for that  old tip number one and pop them in the  knees  tip three sometimes it's okay to run and  no  it does not mean you're a coward what's  wrong mcfly  chicken the ghoulie dudes in particular  are slow and don't prance around like  the lycans  so sometimes it's better to save your  resources and simply go around them to  get to where you're going  now in classic risk reward fashion  killing them  does reward you lay the in-game currency  so just consider  resources and environment before getting  into an altercation  and as a bonus tip it's still okay to  run but set a mind down while you're at  it  bullets and a knife aren't your only  answers to a fight in some scenarios you  will be surrounded on all sides  so turning and running is the only way  to make some distance between you  and the enemy and while you're at it set  a mine down if you have one handy then  scurry off screw it throw a bite bomb at  them too  [Applause]  on to exploration being thorough is the  name of the game here  as it's vital to finding many things  from supplies for crafting  treasure for selling and ammunition that  is pivotal  in your survival and with each area  being pretty dense and even maze-like at  times  it pays off to take your time to scour  high and low  so tip number one always check your map  before leaving a room  the game has a nifty feature that allows  you to indicate whether you've collected  everything in a room  when it's blue that indicates the room  is clear and you've collected everything  otherwise it'll appear red if you're  having trouble clearing a room make sure  to look up  the ceiling for anything that sparkles  if you see it shoot it  these are crystals that can be sold to  the duke the jolly old merchant guy  some rooms will require a lockpick to  complete finishing or a locked door but  thankfully your map marks them once you  find them so it's easy to go back and  clear them out  which is very very nice lastly keep in  mind that there are some  areas you won't be able to return to so  again  it pays to be thorough along your  journey opposed to your map filled with  uncleared rooms and it's awful  tip 2 backtrack it's worth it while a  few areas won't be available later the  actual village  is an area you'll be returning to many  many times  the village is a connecting hub between  each major area of the game  one way or another you'll be returning  there and each time you'll return with  something that can likely  unlock something else whether it's a  wheel for a well a lever four bridge  lock picks or you know keys so before  trudging into the next new area  make sure to take a moment and circle  back around with your newly found  items but like anything else relative to  resident evil  be wary of the risk reward as sometimes  being a thorough player will reward you  with a big old treasure  or a new special gun but it also likely  means you may have to deal with an  onslaught of baddies ready to drain you  of your resources  tip three go hunting it definitely  doesn't feel great killing pigs and  goats just minding their own business in  an already dreadful village infested  with werewolves running around  but as it turns out the true enemy is in  fact  actually ethan winters  killing animals and taking their parts  to the duke has some big  benefits like making meals that will  permanently increase health  block resistance etc also be on the  lookout of pictures of special  animals hidden around the village  hunting these things down  are needed for creating certain special  recipes and while you're on a killing  spree make sure to shoot the crows too  as they drop extra lay as well the last  section  the duke tip 1 check that treasure  before you  sell it selling treasure is an important  loop of the game  giving you a bump in the funds needed to  acquire valuable upgrades  ammunition and health resources if  you're a resident evil 4 veteran  this is a near copy and paste version of  how that one worked  but before you sell something make sure  it isn't combinable with another piece  of treasure  this is thankfully indicated in its  description listed next to its value  finding the piece of treasure it  combines with greatly increases its  value  so consider holding off until you find  its combinable part  tip number two sell your guns as the  game progresses you'll naturally find  better versions of your arsenal along  the way  while you may have grown a sentimental  attachment to your starting pistol and  have  upgraded it all the way it takes up  inventory space  and as the game goes on that space will  become more and more scarce  so just sell it and use that lay to  upgrade your new guard  and while you're at it just sell the  ammo for weapons you don't use as  often and use that money to replenish  the weapons you use a lot  keep in mind that the duke's inventory  is also limited  but it does replenish with every new  area  so here are the big takeaways go for the  knees be thorough  and crows carry plenty of pocket change  be sure to check out all of our other  resident evil coverage  including our review and a detailed  timeline on the entire franchise which  is  quite a wild watch so links in the  description below this was kurt  thanks for watching and happy surviving  you
 

